  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM EDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nDouble barrel shortwave setup traversing the broader Great Lakes and \nOhio Valley regions... a primary synoptic shortwave and associated \noccluded surface low progressing east across northern Ontario, and a \nsecond, convectively charged wave currently sparking deep convection \nwithin a mesolow complex across northern Ohio. Deeper moisture to \nsubtly intrude over the next few hours following the passage of a \nsurface warm frontal boundary (which has sent surface dewpoints into \nthe upper 60s-lower 70s across the board). \n\nNext piece of the puzzle is ongoing destabilization across the \nregion, aided in part by partial clearing just ahead of a surface \ncold frontal boundary draped from roughly the Soo, south and west to \nGreen Bay through Dubuque, IA. Anticipation is that another \nconvective response is to manifest across the region... and \nconvective showers are already trying to fester over eastern\nupper and far northern lower. Peak heating (1,000-1,500 J/kg \nCAPE) coupled with ample moisture (PWATs spiking over 1.75\") \nwill yield efficient rainmakers, though with bulk shear \ngenerally holding sub 30kts, organized severe storms seem \nunlikely. Predominant storm mode is likely to be a disorganized \nmulticellular mode, with the only risk being sub-severe hail and\nsome gusty winds (if deeper convection can materialize), \nparticularly across northeast lower, where the aforementioned \nmeteorological parameters are overlapped and maximized. Given \nthe atmosphere in place, can easily see localized 1.00\"+ \nrainfall amounts, most prevalent across NE lower. The farther \nwest one goes in northern lower, the overall chances of seeing \nrainfall in any given location decrease considerably, especially\nonce one gets west of I-75.\n\nConvective processes to dwindle through the evening, with slow \nclearing overnight as low level moisture holds steadfast, while we \ndry in the upper levels. As surface high pressure moves to our \nsouthwest tomorrow, low level moisture will scour out considering \nreinforced NW flow. Result is a dry Monday with highs in the 70s \nwest into eastern upper, and highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s in \nnortheast lower (with much lower relative humidity!). Dry weather \nwill be short lived as another wave progresses through the region \nlater Tuesday, bringing the next chance for some scattered showers \nand thunderstorms. Again I state... this is not a widespread \nrainfall setup either, so not everyone is going to see rain! In the \nwake of this system, a deeper longwave trough builds into the \nnortheastern part of North America, with the Great Lakes right on \nthe western edge of the feature. Result will be a cooler area of \nhigh pressure settling in to end the week... and with limited \nmoisture return, rain chances will be few and far between through \nthe upcoming weekend, amid a stretch of temperatures holding in the \nlow to upper 70s Wednesday through Friday, moderating into the upper \n70s-low 80s by the weekend.",
